Introduction 
In 2019, bacterial antimicrobial resistance (AMR) was linked to an estimated 4.95 million deaths globally. 
Of these, 1.27 million were directly caused by AMR, with Escherichia coli standing out as the primary pathogen (Murray et al., 2022).
The burden is especially heavy in Western sub-Saharan Africa, which recorded the highest all-age death rate at 27.3 per 100,000. However, tracking this exact impact is difficult because data from low- and middle-income countries (LMICs) is still very fragmented (Murray et al., 2022).
Within Nigeria, the exact toll is severe; the Nigeria Centre for Disease Control and Prevention (NCDC) estimates AMR causes around 263,400 deaths every year (NCDC, 2024). To tackle this, Nigeria developed its first National Action Plan (2017–2022) based on a 2016 situational analysis (Achi et al., 2021). The government has now updated this approach with a second strategic framework running from 2024 to 2028 (NCDC, 2024).

E. coli remains  an important focus for One Health surveillance because it colonises human and animal hosts and crosses ecological boundaries (Founou et al., 2016). It carries mobile genetic elements, including plasmids, transposons and integrons, that facilitate the dissemination of resistance genes between hosts and environments (Poirel et al., 2018). A review of E. coli pathotypes in Nigeria reports ESBL production, including blaCTX-M-15, across human, animal and environmental settings (Anueyiagu et al., 2024). This widespread resistance is heavily driven by unregulated antibiotic use and poor drug quality. For instance, antibiotic  misuse and high tetracycline residues have been reported from studies  focused on aquaculture environments in Northern Nigeria (Alhaji et al., 2024), while the circulation of degraded or fake veterinary antibiotics serves as another major, often overlooked, driver for AMR across the country (Alhassan, 2026)."


Within clinical settings, the AMR by E. coli has been tied to their Extended spectrum  beta latamse production.  For instance, in a tertiary hospital in Enugu, 35% of 200 E. coli isolates were identified as ESBL producers, with infection heavily associated with prior antibiotic usage, intensive care admission, and recent surgery (Nwafia et al 2019.. This high clinical prevalence is mirrored in symptomatic community infections; a survey in Minna reported a 37.7% prevalence of diarrheagenic E. coli harboring multi-locus ESBL genes, even though the specific allelic variants were not sequenced (Iseghohi et al., 2021). Notably, the prevalence of these resistance genes can fluctuate sharply across different hosts, reflecting varying levels of antibiotic selection pressure. This dynamic is  typified in a study involving , an analysis of 114 paired human and bovine isolates in Oyo State detected the CTX-M-15 enzyme exclusively within the human clinical samples, whereas the bovine isolates exhibited no ESBL production (Inwezerua et al., 2014).
In the same manner, multidrug resistance (MDR) has permeated Nigeria’s agricultural and domestic animal environments. Alarmingly high MDR rates have been documented across diverse regional settings, ranging from  94% among 36 isolates from a poultry farm environment in the southwest (Adelowo et al., 2014) to , 92% of 50 abattoir isolates in the northwest (Shiaka et al., 2020), and 70.4% of 71 isolates recovered from healthy chickens and their handlers in Abeokuta (Ajibola et al. 2025). Molecular studies indicate that these agricultural reservoirs are both diverse and keeps changing sas these isolates keeps interacting with contaminated environment. In Lagos, pulsed-field gel electrophoresis (PFGE) of 211 β-lactamase-producing isolates from food animals revealed high overall genetic diversity alongside identical clones circulating across different animal groups, suggesting active cross-species transmission (Adenipekun et al., 2015). This genetic mobility is further evidenced by 162 isolates from healthy chickens and pigs in Ibadan, which carried plasmid-mediated quinolone resistance genes, such as qnrS1 and qnrB19,across a variety of plasmids (Fortini et al., 2011). Advanced genomic surveillance continues to highlight the complexity of these environments, with whole-genome sequencing of 47 isolates from commercial poultry farms in Oyo State confirming the circulation of multiple ESBL variants, including blaCTX-M-15, -27, -55, and -65 (Adetunji et al. 2025). Furthermore, this resistance reservoir extends beyond agricultural boundaries into companion animals, as demonstrated by the phenotypic detection of resistant E. coli isolated from 100 apparently healthy dogs in Jos (Pam et al., 2026).
Environmental studies also indict sewage and waste water systems, that sustains the AMR cycle. This can be seen in an investigation of abattoir wastewater in Calabar South, which showed a  62.4% E. coli prevalence, with 99.8% of isolates being multidrug-resistant (Agashi et al., 2025). This resistance burden is readily disseminated into broader aquatic ecosystems. In Edo State, 114 surface water isolates included ESBL producers carrying blaCTX-M-1, blaCTX-M-15, and class 1 and 2 integrons (Beshiru et al., 2024). Similarly, E. coli O157:H7 from Osun State freshwater frequently harbored the blaTEM gene (Bisi-Johnson et al., 2023). Corroborating this widespread environmental persistence, multidrug-resistant E. coli were also recovered across various enviromnetal sources in Nasarawa State (Rebecca et al., 2023).

The aggregation of the aforementioned studies reiterates the strong presence of resistant E. coli strains across the major domains pertinent to One Health in Nigeria. However, they provide little information on the interrelationship of these isolates, as previous research is often limited by a reliance on phenotypic testing, inadequate sample sizes, or a lack of genomic analysis (Medugu et al., 2025). Existing genomic studies are mostly clinical, such as the analysis of 107 multidrug-resistant isolates from a tertiary hospital (Medugu et al., 2025) and bloodstream isolates from sentinel sites (Okolie et al., 2023). Currently, there is a distinct paucity of data comparing isolates across all three One Health sectors at the genomic level.
To address this critical genomic surveillance gap, this study leverages on the  publicly available whole-genome sequence data to conduct a cross-sectoral bioinformatic analysis of Nigerian E. coli strains from human clinical, abattoir, and environmental sources across Lagos, Ibadan, and Edo State. Going beyond conventional molecular methods, it applies advanced computational pipelines to profile antimicrobial resistance genes and predict antimicrobial peptides. This study aims to characterize the distribution of resistance determinants across distinct One Health settings and inform national mitigation strategies.

